@meta
id: 32-11
title: Review — the comment you do not apply
chapter: 32
type: scenario
skills: code-review, sub-basic, ex-sort, open-O
par: 128
minutes: 11
vscode: apply all four suggestions, break the API
open: src/domain/order/order.provider.ts src/domain/order/order.resolver.ts src/domain/order/order.schema.graphql
vimrc: set hidden

@brief
Four comments on !341. Three of them you apply. One you do not.

    @jm  Three `let`s in here that are never reassigned. `const`.

    @jm  `50000` twice. Pull it out as `APPROVAL_LIMIT` at module
         scope, under the imports, one blank line either side, and use
         the name in both places.

    @pv  The imports in the resolver are not in order. Sort them.

    @pv  `args.id` reads badly in the resolver. Rename it to
         `args.orderId`.

The last one is wrong, and the schema file open in the third buffer is
why: `id` is the argument name in the published GraphQL schema. Renaming
the destructured field renames the API. Every client breaks, and the
reviewer cannot see that from the diff they were looking at.

So: the provider and the resolver's imports change. Nothing else in the
resolver changes, and the schema does not change. Reply to @pv with the
schema line; that part is not something vim can do for you.

@keys
| `:%s/\<let\>/const/g` | whole word only, every line |
| `:1,4sort` | sort those four lines |
| `O` | open a line above and type on it |
| `:bn` `:wqa` | next buffer, write everything |

@hints
Do the two substitutions in the provider before you type the constant.
If you add `const APPROVAL_LIMIT = 50000;` first, the substitution that
replaces `50000` will replace it in the declaration too, and you will
have written `const APPROVAL_LIMIT = APPROVAL_LIMIT;`.
--
`\<let\>` with the word boundaries, not bare `let`. Without them the
pattern is inside `complete`, `deleted`, `outlet` and anything else that
happens to contain the letters.
--
For the constant: `:%s/50000/APPROVAL_LIMIT/g` first, then `/^@injectable`
`Enter` `O`, type the declaration, press Enter once more to leave a blank
line, and `Esc`.

@solution
`:%s/\<let\>/const/g`, `:%s/50000/APPROVAL_LIMIT/g`,
`/^@injectable` `Enter` `O` `const APPROVAL_LIMIT = 50000;` `Enter` `Esc`,
then `:bn` `:1,4sort` `:wqa`. 111 keystrokes.

**Order of operations is the trap.** Both substitutions are trivial, and
doing them in the wrong order relative to typing the declaration produces
a file that compiles, reads almost right, and is wrong. When you are
about to introduce a name that is defined in terms of the thing you are
replacing, replace first and declare after. It is the same reason you
rename the callers before the definition when the compiler is helping
you.

**`\<` and `\>` are not decoration.** `let` is three letters that occur
inside real words. A rename of a keyword or a short identifier without
word boundaries is one of the two or three ways a careless refactor gets
into a codebase, and it is invisible in review because the diff looks
plausible.

**`:sort` for imports.** Four lines, one command, no argument about which
order they were in before. Note that it is a plain ASCII sort, so
`tsyringe` sorts after the capitalised ones — which is the convention
this codebase already uses everywhere else, so it happens to be right.
Check that before you reach for it in a project that groups its imports
by origin; there `:sort` will cheerfully destroy the grouping.

**And the one you do not apply.** The interesting part of this lesson is
the comment you leave alone. A reviewer reading a diff cannot see the
schema file, and a suggestion that is right about the code can still be
wrong about the system. Applying all four is faster, feels more
agreeable, and ships an API break. "No, because" plus a file path is a
complete answer and takes thirty seconds to write.

@start src/domain/order/order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    let order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    let order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.total > 50000) {
      throw exception('REFUND_NEEDS_APPROVAL');
    }

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }

  public async refund(id: string, amount: number) {
    let order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (amount > 50000) {
      throw exception('REFUND_NEEDS_APPROVAL');
    }

    return this.paymentService.refund(order.id, amount);
  }
}

@expected src/domain/order/order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

const APPROVAL_LIMIT = 50000;

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.total > APPROVAL_LIMIT) {
      throw exception('REFUND_NEEDS_APPROVAL');
    }

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }

  public async refund(id: string, amount: number) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (amount > APPROVAL_LIMIT) {
      throw exception('REFUND_NEEDS_APPROVAL');
    }

    return this.paymentService.refund(order.id, amount);
  }
}

@start src/domain/order/order.resolver.ts
import { OrderProvider } from './order.provider';
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { CustomerProvider } from '../customer/customer.provider';
import { InvoiceProvider } from '../billing/invoice.provider';

@injectable()
export class OrderResolver {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderProvider) protected orderProvider: OrderProvider,
    @inject(CustomerProvider) protected customerProvider: CustomerProvider,
    @inject(InvoiceProvider) protected invoiceProvider: InvoiceProvider,
  ) {}

  public async order(_: unknown, args: { id: string }) {
    return this.orderProvider.findOne(args.id);
  }

  public async cancelOrder(_: unknown, args: { id: string }) {
    return this.orderProvider.cancel(args.id);
  }

  public async orderInvoice(_: unknown, args: { id: string }) {
    return this.invoiceProvider.forOrder(args.id);
  }
}

@expected src/domain/order/order.resolver.ts
import { CustomerProvider } from '../customer/customer.provider';
import { InvoiceProvider } from '../billing/invoice.provider';
import { OrderProvider } from './order.provider';
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';

@injectable()
export class OrderResolver {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderProvider) protected orderProvider: OrderProvider,
    @inject(CustomerProvider) protected customerProvider: CustomerProvider,
    @inject(InvoiceProvider) protected invoiceProvider: InvoiceProvider,
  ) {}

  public async order(_: unknown, args: { id: string }) {
    return this.orderProvider.findOne(args.id);
  }

  public async cancelOrder(_: unknown, args: { id: string }) {
    return this.orderProvider.cancel(args.id);
  }

  public async orderInvoice(_: unknown, args: { id: string }) {
    return this.invoiceProvider.forOrder(args.id);
  }
}

@start src/domain/order/order.schema.graphql
type Query {
  order(id: ID!): Order
  orderInvoice(id: ID!): Invoice
}

type Mutation {
  cancelOrder(id: ID!): Order
}
